From: François Guerraz <kubrick@fgv6.net>
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: qualcomm: ppe: emulate the rtl8_4 head tag 
 with egress-only TPIDs

The nexthop of a flow towards a DSA user port inserts an S-tag (TCI
0x0400) and a C-tag (TCI BIT(port)), which reproduce the RTL8372N's
head tag only when the chip-wide egress S-TPID is 0x8899 and the conduit
is an S-VLAN egress port. Kamil Bienkiewicz's parser alias (his 0445 and
0449) does that and also reprograms the ingress TPIDs, under which a
tagged WAN is no longer parsed (measured by him: its flows never hit).

Set only the egress half, profile E: EG_VLAN_TPID's S-TPID to 0x8899,
its C-TPID checked at 0x8100, and the conduit's PORT_EG_VLAN to S-VLAN
type with PCP and DEI propagation off (nothing of the WAN's tag may land
in the head tag's protocol byte or port mask). Ingress TPIDs and port
roles stay at reset, so every port keeps its RSS and receive checksum;
uploads stay in software. By the reading of ppe_servcode_init(), CPU
transmits bypass egress VLAN editing, so the software path does not see
the S-TPID (gate HE1).

Flows from the switch are now declined: with the reset ingress TPIDs the
parser stops at the head tag, so the entries the previous patch
programmed for them never hit. A flow towards a DSA port is accepted
only when the switch bridges that port in the Linux bridge it is in
(dsa_port_bridge_dev_get() is that bridge): a port the switch driver
leaves to software bridging (a refused join, a VLAN upper) is standalone
in hardware. The FDB follower applies the same test to the port it
re-points to. The bridge's MTU caps the flow's PMTU, the packets being
routed through it.

E is applied at enable when an rtl8_4 conduit exists, otherwise on the
first flow towards one, saved and read back first; a failure keeps flows
towards the switch declined until disable. It is left only by disable,
after every flow entry is confirmed deleted.

The head tag adds 8 bytes on the conduit at zero margin; the port's MTU
failure action is already redirect to the CPU while offload is on.

The comment in edma_port_feature_check() (from 0431) that no TPID
register is programmed now says that the parser's are not, and the
head-tag comment above PPE_OFFLOAD_RTL8_4_STAG_TCI (from 0434) points at
the propagation bits E turns off.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
